short-code {"wall_s": 6.516100625000002, "ttft_s": 0.4486967919999998, "text": "# LRU Cache Implementation\n\nBelow is a complete LRU cache built with a doubly linked list + dictionary, plus a full unit test suite.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"LRU cache backed by a doubly linked list and a hash map.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Hashable, Optional\n\n\nclass _Node:\n    \"\"\"Doubly linked list node holding a cache entry.\"\"\"\n\n    __slots__ = (\"key\", \"value\", \"prev\", \"next\")\n\n    def __init__(self, key: Hashable, value: Any) -> None:\n        self.key: Hashable = key\n        self.value: Any = value\n        self.prev: Optional[\"_Node\"] = None\n        self.next: Optional[\"_Node\"] = None\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:  # pragma: no cover - debugging aid\n        return f\"_Node({self.key!r}, {self.value!r})\"\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    Least-Recently-Used cache with O(1) get/put.\n\n    Invariants:\n      - head <-> ... <-> tail (sentinels); head.next is the MRU entry,\n        tail.prev is the LRU entry.\n      - self._map maps", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 56, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 312, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 56}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-code", "thinking": false}
short-story {"wall_s": 7.8165717080000015, "ttft_s": 0.4283976669999987, "text": "# The Keeper of Point Adelaid\n\nMira Voss had kept the light at Point Adelaid for eleven years, and in eleven years she had learned that the sea spoke in three ways: the crash of breakers, the low moan of fog, and the silence between them. Tonight it spoke in the fourth way\u2014a sound she had no name for, like a bell rung underwater, heard from the inside.\n\nThe message arrived at 3:14 in the morning, printed on the weather teletype that had been obsolete since before she took the post. The machine should have been dead. It should have been rust. But the paper fed through clean and crisp, and the type was sharp as a scalpel.\n\n**TO KEEPER VOSS. STORM FRONT UNREPORTED. SQUALL LINE WILL CROSS YOUR POINT AT 0447. FISHING VESSEL DELPHIN OUT OF HARBOR GRACE, THREE ABOARD, WILL BE IN ITS PATH. THEY WILL NOT HEAR YOUR HORN. LIGHT ONLY. LIGHT WILL SAVE THEM. CONFIRM RECEIPT.**\n\nMira read it twice. Then a third time, standing in the lamplight of the watch room with her hair half undone and", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 57, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 313, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 57}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-story", "thinking": false}
short-reasoning {"wall_s": 6.988954875000001, "ttft_s": 0.49075316700000116, "text": "**Setting up the calculation**\n\nTotal capacity needed: 120 requests/second.\n\nEach worker handles 20 requests/second, so raw worker count:\n\n120 \u00f7 20 = **6 workers**\n\n**Applying the reserve**\n\nBut we must reserve 25% of *total capacity* for spikes \u2014 meaning only 75% of the workers' capacity can be used for regular traffic. So the usable capacity per worker is effectively:\n\n20 \u00d7 0.75 = 15 requests/second per worker\n\nNow solve:\n\n120 \u00f7 15 = **8 workers**\n\n**Check the result**\n\n- 8 workers \u00d7 20 req/s = 160 req/s total capacity\n- Reserve: 25% \u00d7 160 = 40 req/s held for spikes \u2713\n- Regular traffic: 160 \u2212 40 = 120 req/s \u2713 \u2014 exactly matches the load\n\n**Answer: 8 workers**\n\nNote the trap: naively doing 120 \u00f7 20 = 6 then adding 25% more (6 \u00d7 1.25 = 7.5 \u2192 7.5 workers) gives the wrong answer, because reserving 25% of *capacity* is not the same as adding 25% more workers. With 8 workers", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 70, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 326, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 70}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-reasoning", "thinking": false}
